1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,098
At the South Pole, this building hides
the scale of what is underneath it.

2
00:00:04,377 --> 00:00:07,639
Below the snow, thousands
of light sensors are frozen

3
00:00:07,697 --> 00:00:10,530
into a cubic kilometer of Antarctic ice.

4
00:00:10,855 --> 00:00:16,417
Together, they form IceCube: an observatory
built to catch particles from space.

5
00:00:16,626 --> 00:00:19,308
But this telescope has no giant mirror.

6
00:00:19,586 --> 00:00:24,080
And the particles it is waiting
for usually pass straight through matter.

7
00:00:24,463 --> 00:00:25,867
So why build it here?

8
00:00:26,111 --> 00:00:30,616
And how do you bury an instrument
this large without excavating the ice?

9
00:00:30,894 --> 00:00:33,715
Start at the surface, then go down.

10
00:00:34,830 --> 00:00:39,358
The original deep array begins about fourteen
hundred and fifty meters below the ice

11
00:00:39,474 --> 00:00:42,667
and extends to roughly twenty-four
hundred and fifty meters.

12
00:00:43,108 --> 00:00:48,542
Eighty-six cables carry five thousand,
one hundred and sixty optical modules.

13
00:00:48,867 --> 00:00:52,559
That is the original detector,
completed in twenty ten.

14
00:00:52,942 --> 00:00:57,586
The illustration shows its scale,
not every sensor's exact position.

15
00:00:58,027 --> 00:01:00,639
The building above
is only the visible part.

16
00:01:01,243 --> 00:01:03,890
The detector includes the ice itself.

17
00:01:04,331 --> 00:01:08,917
Neutrinos are electrically neutral
particles with extremely small masses.

18
00:01:09,091 --> 00:01:13,689
They interact so rarely that enormous
numbers pass through us unnoticed.

19
00:01:14,199 --> 00:01:18,936
IceCube's own fact sheet puts that number
at about a hundred trillion every second.

20
00:01:19,633 --> 00:01:23,093
For a detector, that creates
an unusual problem.

21
00:01:23,418 --> 00:01:28,642
A particle can cross your instrument
and leave absolutely nothing to record.

22
00:01:29,153 --> 00:01:32,752
Making the target larger increases
the opportunity for one

23
00:01:32,787 --> 00:01:34,656
of those rare interactions.

24
00:01:35,167 --> 00:01:39,660
Here, the target is an enormous
volume of clear, stable ice.

25
00:01:39,985 --> 00:01:43,236
So the challenge is not simply
seeing deeper into space.

26
00:01:43,619 --> 00:01:47,137
It is catching a messenger
that normally refuses to stop.

27
00:01:47,648 --> 00:01:52,617
To install the sensors, teams used hot
water to melt deep holes into the ice.

28
00:01:53,127 --> 00:01:56,587
They lowered cables of optical
modules into those holes,

29
00:01:56,761 --> 00:01:58,932
then let the water freeze around them.

30
00:01:59,443 --> 00:02:01,382
Imagine the sequence in reverse.

31
00:02:01,707 --> 00:02:04,285
A buried sensor leads up to a cable.

32
00:02:04,563 --> 00:02:06,398
The cable leads to a hole.

33
00:02:06,781 --> 00:02:09,300
And that hole had to be made
from the surface,

34
00:02:09,509 --> 00:02:11,866
through more than two kilometers of ice.

35
00:02:12,249 --> 00:02:15,303
Construction took place
over Antarctic summer seasons

36
00:02:15,372 --> 00:02:17,834
between twenty oh four and twenty ten.

37
00:02:18,275 --> 00:02:21,989
The original deep array ended
up with eighty-six strings,

38
00:02:22,140 --> 00:02:24,068
each holding sixty modules.

39
00:02:24,393 --> 00:02:25,914
The multiplication is simple.

40
00:02:26,297 --> 00:02:29,106
Building the result
at the South Pole was not.

41
00:02:29,489 --> 00:02:32,752
There is no underground hall
surrounding this instrument.

42
00:02:33,077 --> 00:02:37,570
The sensors are frozen into the material
they use to detect particles.

43
00:02:37,571 --> 00:02:40,276
Now follow one neutrino into the detector.

44
00:02:40,659 --> 00:02:42,215
Usually, nothing happens.

45
00:02:42,424 --> 00:02:46,418
Occasionally, it interacts
and produces charged particles.

46
00:02:46,743 --> 00:02:51,956
Those secondary particles can emit a faint
blue light called Cherenkov radiation

47
00:02:52,130 --> 00:02:53,871
as they travel through the ice.

48
00:02:54,196 --> 00:02:57,099
They can move faster
than light travels in ice.

49
00:02:57,424 --> 00:03:00,965
That does not mean they break
the speed limit of light in a vacuum.

50
00:03:01,406 --> 00:03:03,577
The sensors record the arriving light.

51
00:03:03,960 --> 00:03:07,966
Their timing and positions allow
scientists to reconstruct the event.

52
00:03:08,210 --> 00:03:09,626
For a track-like event,

53
00:03:09,800 --> 00:03:13,666
the pattern helps estimate
the direction of the incoming neutrino.

54
00:03:13,992 --> 00:03:18,345
The glowing dots in this animation
are a diagram of detector responses.

55
00:03:18,519 --> 00:03:22,409
They are not a camera watching
a giant beam shine underground.

56
00:03:22,792 --> 00:03:26,287
The colors indicate the order
in which light reaches sensors.

57
00:03:26,728 --> 00:03:28,458
That distinction matters.

58
00:03:28,968 --> 00:03:32,707
IceCube does not take an ordinary
photograph of a neutrino.

59
00:03:33,032 --> 00:03:36,202
It reconstructs an encounter
from the light left behind.

60
00:03:36,899 --> 00:03:40,892
In twenty thirteen, IceCube
announced evidence for a population

61
00:03:40,939 --> 00:03:44,712
of high-energy neutrinos
from beyond our solar system.

62
00:03:45,153 --> 00:03:48,009
That established a new way
to study the universe.

63
00:03:48,520 --> 00:03:52,886
But finding a population is different
from identifying a particular source.

64
00:03:53,582 --> 00:03:57,483
Then, on September twenty-second,
twenty seventeen,

65
00:03:57,924 --> 00:04:03,044
IceCube detected a high-energy event
and sent an alert to astronomers.

66
00:04:03,555 --> 00:04:07,723
Its reconstructed direction narrowed
the search to a region of sky.

67
00:04:08,327 --> 00:04:10,834
Other observatories checked that region.

68
00:04:11,438 --> 00:04:17,394
NASA's Fermi telescope found
that a known blazar, called T X S zero five

69
00:04:17,440 --> 00:04:23,280
zero six, plus zero five six,
was unusually active in gamma rays.

70
00:04:23,721 --> 00:04:28,795
A blazar is an active galaxy
with a powerful jet pointed roughly toward us.

71
00:04:29,306 --> 00:04:34,147
The neutrino direction and the light
observations together provided evidence linking

72
00:04:34,217 --> 00:04:35,668
the event to that source.

73
00:04:36,109 --> 00:04:38,815
The finding was announced
in twenty eighteen.

74
00:04:39,325 --> 00:04:41,949
This was a coordinated investigation,

75
00:04:42,228 --> 00:04:47,267
not IceCube identifying a distant
black hole from one flash alone.

76
00:04:47,650 --> 00:04:50,169
One instrument supplied the particle clue.

77
00:04:50,552 --> 00:04:52,096
Others supplied the light.

78
00:04:52,480 --> 00:04:54,941
That is why the location matters.

79
00:04:55,324 --> 00:04:59,794
At the surface, Antarctica looks
like a place that hides things.

80
00:05:00,119 --> 00:05:04,554
Here, its clear ice becomes part
of an instrument for revealing them.

81
00:05:05,158 --> 00:05:09,721
The original array is also a baseline,
not the end of the project.

82
00:05:10,162 --> 00:05:14,434
IceCube reported a major upgrade
deployment in twenty twenty-six.

83
00:05:14,875 --> 00:05:17,302
That is separate
from the original five thousand,

84
00:05:17,360 --> 00:05:20,158
one hundred and sixty
modules shown earlier.

85
00:05:20,296 --> 00:05:22,885
Return to that small building on the snow.

86
00:05:23,268 --> 00:05:25,462
Now picture the cables
descending beneath it,

87
00:05:25,579 --> 00:05:28,527
and a volume of ice waiting
for a rare interaction.

88
00:05:28,911 --> 00:05:31,140
Most particles pass without a trace.

89
00:05:31,418 --> 00:05:34,646
A few leave enough light
to begin a search across the sky.
